<Project>

  <!-- Capture a .trx for every test project, automatically.

       WHY THIS EXISTS. On 2026-07-28 a merge-result verification run failed 1 of 2667 tests. Four
       re-runs came back green, the two known flake suspects ran clean three times each, and the test
       was NEVER IDENTIFIED — because the failing run left no artifact and by the time an explicit
       trx logger argument was passed on the command line, the failure no longer reproduced. The only
       evidence was a generic
       `MethodBaseInvoker.InvokeWithNoArgs` frame, which names nothing. The change was pushed on a
       judgement call rather than on evidence, and that judgement could have been wrong.

       A .trx carries the failing test NAMES, messages and stack traces — precisely what distinguishes
       "an unrelated test raced on shared state" from "the change under review broke something".

       Set here as an MSBuild property rather than documented as a command-line flag DELIBERATELY: a
       flag has to be remembered by every person and every agent that runs the suite, and it was not
       remembered the one time it mattered. As a property it applies to every `dotnet test` invocation
       of every *.Tests project automatically, including the first run after a merge — which is exactly
       when the evidence went missing.

       The filename is TIMESTAMPED so a green re-run cannot overwrite the red run that preceded it.
       Losing the red run is the specific failure this is here to stop.

       Files accumulate under TestResults/ (gitignored) and can be deleted freely; keep any that came
       from a failing run.

       Passing an explicit logger argument on the command line overrides this (the Condition only sets
       VSTestLogger when it is empty), which is fine for targeted runs. A run narrowed with a filter
       but no explicit logger DOES still leave a trx — verified on adding this, because the sibling
       comment this was adapted from claims otherwise and that claim does not hold here.

       Mirrors the identical convention in the Pellucid repo's Directory.Build.props, which was added
       there for the same reason after two investigations stalled at the same point. This repo had no
       Directory.Build.props at all, so this file also becomes the place for any future repo-wide
       MSBuild settings. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests'))">
    <VSTestResultsDirectory>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)TestResults</VSTestResultsDirectory>
    <VSTestLogger Condition="'$(VSTestLogger)' == ''">trx%3BLogFileName=$(MSBuildProjectName)-$([System.DateTime]::Now.ToString(`yyyyMMdd-HHmmss-fff`)).trx</VSTestLogger>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
